# Engineering Bar

Canonical principles: [`SYSTEM_DESIGN.md`](../../SYSTEM_DESIGN.md).

Assume review by experienced Shopify engineers. Prefer simple architecture. Strong typing. Small modules. Clear service interfaces. Tests with implementation. Never swallow errors. Observable async work. Idempotent external ops.

## Per-feature workflow

1. Understand existing architecture and inspect relevant files.
2. Explain the plan briefly; implement only requested scope.
3. Add/update tests; run tests, lint, and type checks.
4. Review security and the diff.
5. Report exactly what changed and remaining issues.

## Security (non-negotiable)

- Tenant isolation at API, service, DB, and tool layers
- Server-side authz; AI cannot bypass authorization
- Least-privilege Shopify scopes; validate against current official docs before implementing
- Secrets in AWS Secrets Manager — never in source, logs, or the browser
- Tool authorization, input validation, rate limits, prompt-injection defenses
- Audit every important event; never log credentials

## Shopify integration

Real app: OAuth, encrypted tokens, Admin API, webhooks, initial + incremental sync, resource IDs, rate limits, retries, idempotency. Development store support. Official APIs only.

## Quality

No fake/mock functionality when the feature should be real. Mark development-only mocks clearly. Do not modify unrelated files. Environments (`dev` / `staging` / `production`) never share credentials or data.

## Completeness

A feature is done when it works, is tested, typed, handles errors, is observable, is secure, is tenant-scoped, is documented, and can be deployed reproducibly. CI uses FakeLLM; live-model eval is separate.

## Docs

Significant decisions get a new ADR (do not silently rewrite accepted ones). Keep `docs/*` current. Do not add pgvector, Kubernetes, or an execute tool.
